<<~ ahu slot=`#/head`>>

! The receipt ledger ~ what a vessel says about a request, and who else may say anything

A person asks a vessel to act, and the ask carries a request id. This carrier states what the vessel records about that id, what each party may say about it, and what a later read of the id answers. It covers the leg where the asker and the vessel share a machine, and it states what a leg across a herm adds.

> ''Register.'' The operator ruled the three receipts, that a caller's timeout never counts as a cancel, and that no wall clock decides whether an act runs (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings`>>). The operator ruled the control document's retirement as a receipt store: receipts stamp only where an act moves a record, no receipt ring and no receipt record stands, no digest work rides an ordinary save, and a receipt rides the door's answer and the act record. The house settled the law below under those rulings. A line marked ''house reading'' states a choice the rulings leave room for.

The verb, summons and outcome records themselves read at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/verb-tiddler`>>. The socket between a CLI and its vessel reads at <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/api/lares-lararium-binding`>>.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/three-receipts`>>

!! Three receipts ~ three parties, three facts

''A cancel yields three receipts, and none stands in for another.''

| Receipt | Who says it | What it states | What it never states |
| The caller's ''abandonment'' | the asker | "I withdraw request R" | that anything stopped |
| The relay's ''delivery'' | a herm that carried the cancel | "I handed this frame to a socket proven under the key it names" | that the vessel read it, agreed, or acted |
| The vessel's ''outcome'' | the vessel that ran the act | what the vessel holds for R | what the caller meant |

* ''Each party says only the fact it holds.'' The asker holds its own intent. A relay holds transit. The vessel holds the act. A reader who wants the verdict on an act reads the vessel's outcome and nothing else.
* ''A cancel asks; it never commands.'' A cancel that reaches the vessel before it holds anything for the id wins, and the id reads `abandoned`. A cancel that reaches the vessel after it dispatched changes nothing: the act runs to its end.
* ''A caller's timeout never counts as a cancel.'' A wait that ends tells the waiter to stop listening, and it sends no cancel. A person withdraws by an explicit act. What a hang-up does reads at #/abandonment.
* ''Quiet never means a verdict.'' A missing delivery receipt reads as transit unknown, never as "not delivered". A missing outcome reads as `unknown`, never as "did not run".

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-fact`>>

!! What the vessel holds ~ memory for its life, and the act record

No receipt record rests. What the vessel holds about a request id lives in three places, and only the act record outlasts the vessel's life.

| Holder | What it holds | How long |
| the ''answer ring'' (`answerRingOf`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/control-home.ts`) | each verb's whole outcome: status, results, refusal, input digest, the change report | memory alone; a count and a byte bound, the oldest leaving first; a restart empties it |
| the ''ask book'' (`AskBook`, `packages/lararium-node/src/uds-channel/ask-book.ts`) | per request id: an ask still `running` (with its input digest), an id the door `abandoned` before dispatch, a landed ask's input digest, a session act's verdict (its answer rode the socket alone) | memory alone; one count bound, the oldest leaving first; a restart empties it |
| the ''act record'' in the control document | a write's ''verdict row'' (`verdictRowOf`: status, digest, refusal, the measure of the body, never the body) and, under an effect key, the ''effect row'' naming the attempt and its digest | rests with the vessel, the newest `VERDICT_ROW_COUNT` rows kept (#/past-memory); a read leaves none |

* ''One synchronous turn decides.'' The door reads the ring and the book and books an id `running` in one turn, so two asks under one id never both run. No write, flush or clock decides.
* ''The input digest names what the request asked'': verb, arguments, face and effect key (`verbInputDigest`, `packages/lararium-mesh/src/verb-tiddler.ts`). The worker stamps the same digest on the outcome, the verdict row and the effect row.
* ''An outcome that lands answers, whatever road ran the verb.'' A summons under an id the door abandoned may still run on the summons road; its outcome then answers both doors.
* ''A life holds its own memory.'' A request a vessel life dispatched and never saw land leaves no record: the next life names no state for it (`unknown`), and an effect key dedups its re-ask at the island's effect row.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/one-winner`>>

!! One winner per request id

A request id names one attempt. The door hands the verb to the worker once per id per life. A second line under an id the vessel still holds never runs:

| The vessel holds | A dispatch under that id answers |
| an answer in the ring, or a write's verdict row, same digest | that outcome, read back (a body the ring let go reads `body: {released, sha256, bytes, cure}`) |
| a running ask, same digest | the outcome once it lands, read again; `unknown` where the vessel's own wait for it ends |
| any of the above, other digest | refusal `request-id-reused-with-changed-input` |
| an abandonment | refusal `abandoned` |
| nothing (another life, or a read whose answer left the ring) | a fresh run: a read changes nothing, and an effect key dedups a write at the effect row |

A ''receipt read'' asks what the vessel holds and dispatches nothing. It answers `done` or `error` where an outcome stands, `committed` for a running ask, `abandoned`, or `unknown` for an id nothing names. An outcome that stands answers its verdict even where the book reads `abandoned`: a record that landed says the act ran, on whatever road.

''The effect key names the change, the request id names the attempt.'' A retry under a fresh id and the same effect key meets the worker's effect index (`packages/lararium-tw5/src/verb-dispatcher.ts`). Where the effect row names the same input digest, the retry draws that result (whole where the island's ring still holds it, by its measure where the body left) and runs nothing twice. Under another digest, or beside a row naming no digest, it runs as a fresh change.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/abandonment`>>

!! Abandonment ~ a causal event, never a timer

Two events abandon a request, and each happens before the vessel commits:

# ''The asker hung up before the vessel read the line.'' The vessel finds the connection ended when it first turns to the request, marks the id `abandoned` in its ask book, and runs nothing. A read whose caller left marks nothing: no effect could land twice.
# ''A person cancelled the id'' before the vessel held anything for it. The ask book marks it `abandoned`, with no digest, since a cancel carries no content.

An abandonment lives in memory for the vessel's life, as every ask-book entry does.

Three things never abandon a request:

* ''The asker's wait ended'', taken alone. The asker stops listening, closes its connection and keeps the request id. No cancel crosses. Where the vessel committed before it met that close, the act runs on. Where the vessel first turns to the request after the close, event 1 above abandons it: the close decides, as an event the vessel met before commit, and the timer that caused it decides nothing at the vessel. Either way a later receipt read answers which.
* ''The vessel's own wait for an outcome ended.'' The vessel answers `unknown` on that connection, and the act runs on.
* ''The asker hung up after the commit.'' The act runs to its end, and its outcome waits for a read.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/past-memory`>>

!! Past memory ~ what a re-ask reads once the ring let go

The answer ring and the ask book each hold a count, and neither reads a clock. Past them, a re-ask meets what rests:

* ''A write'' left its verdict row: while the row stands, the re-ask reads the verdict, its body released with the measure and the cure, and runs nothing.
* ''An effect'' left its effect row: a retry under a fresh id draws that result by its digest and runs nothing twice.
* ''A read'' left nothing: the re-ask runs again, and changes nothing.

''The act records hold a count.'' The control document keeps the newest `VERDICT_ROW_COUNT` verdict rows (four answer rings' worth); past it the oldest batch leaves whole, each row with the effect row that still names its request, by retract in one change (`packages/lararium-node/src/uds-channel/row-bound.ts`). A count, never a clock or a dial: every change to the document costs per title it holds. A re-ask past the kept rows runs fresh, or refuses on a changed input the ask book still holds.

No `evicted`, `interrupted` or torn state answers: no receipt rests to evict, interrupt or tear.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-direct-leg`>>

!! The direct leg ~ a CLI beside its vessel

The CLI and the vessel share a machine and meet at the vessel's owner-only socket. One line asks, one line answers.

| A person runs | The line | The vessel answers |
| `lares act …` and every verb command | the verb, its arguments, the requester, a request id the CLI minted | the outcome, a named refusal, or `unknown` where the vessel's own wait ended |
| `lares vessel request <id>` | `{op:"receipt"}` | what the vessel holds for the id |
| `lares vessel request <id> --cancel` | `{op:"cancel"}` | what a receipt read answers after the cancel took its turn: `abandoned` where the cancel stands, else what the vessel holds for the id |

On this leg two of the three receipts exist. The cancel line carries the caller's abandonment, and the answer carries the vessel's outcome. No relay stands between them, so no delivery receipt exists.

''`lares vessel request --cancel` reports both.'' A cancel that stands reads `withdrawn: true` and exits clean. A cancel that met a commit reads `withdrawn: false` with the code `cancel-lost` and the state the vessel holds, and the act runs on. A line from the vessel that names no state reads as a fault on both doors, never as a verdict.

''The CLI mints the request id before it sends'', so the id survives a lost answer. A CLI whose wait ended reports `outcome-unknown` with the id, reads the receipt once by that id, and never sends the verb a second time.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/the-relay-leg`>>

!! The relay leg ~ what a herm between asker and vessel adds

A herm carries sealed frames between keys it proved, reads none of them, and holds no reading of the act (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/vessel-caps`>>). An ask that crosses a herm adds one party and one receipt.

''What the law fixes:''

* ''One door, whatever the leg.'' The vessel that runs the act holds the only reading of it. An ask that arrives across a herm meets the same arbitration, the same refusals and the same memory as an ask from the socket. The outcome that crosses back states that vessel's reading.
* ''The relay's receipt attests transit only.'' A herm states that it handed a frame to a socket proven under the key the frame names. It arbitrates nothing, keeps nothing, and its word never moves the vessel's reading or the asker's reading of the act.
* ''A herm's stamp never grants authority.'' The herm stamps each frame with the key its sender proved. That stamp routes the answer home. It never licenses a verb: the vessel reads the asker's own proof (''house reading'', from carry ⊥ read ⊥ contract).
* ''No timer on any side.'' The asker's wait, the herm's socket deadline and the vessel's wait each pace their own holder. None of them abandons, cancels or evicts.
* ''The asker keeps its own abandonment.'' A cancel that the herm drops still happened on the asker's side. The asker records that it withdrew, and reads the vessel's outcome when one reaches it.

''What stays owed.'' No code asks, cancels or answers across a herm. A remote caller reaches a vessel by dialing that vessel's own daemon door and writing a summons (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/verb-tiddler#/contract`>>); a herm carries no daemon document, since its carriage moves sealed envelopes and the document relay stays a socket of its own (`packages/lararium-node/src/carriage-relay.ts`). A summoned verb's outcome answers the door like any other, and a summoner holds no cancel.

''What a herm already carries.'' An envelope holds an open `kind` and an opaque payload, and the relay routes it between proven keys (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/membership-channel.ts`). Two siblings under one PersonaGroup hold a sealed session across a herm (`packages/lararium-mesh/src/sibling-channel.ts`). Either could carry an ask with no change to the frame a herm routes. Neither gives the ask a meaning, a reader at the vessel, or an authority; and a herm sends nothing back when it hands a frame onward.

''The relay leg's three shapes'' (house ruling under the operator's delegation; <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/rulings`>>). Each answers one need, and none adds a signing domain.

# ''The asker's proof: the sealed sibling session.'' The ask rides the sealed sibling session, which already proved its far end, and the vessel reads that session's proof as the asker's. A herm's stamp never serves. Reach therefore narrows to one PersonaGroup: an asker outside the vessel's own PersonaGroup holds no such session and asks across no herm.
# ''The sealed payload: the session's own seal.'' The ask, the cancel and the vessel's answer ride under that session's seal as a tagged inner frame beside the sync message, so a herm reads no plaintext and the frame it routes stays unchanged.
# ''The delivery receipt: a bare session note, good for the asker alone.'' When the herm hands a frame onward, it sends the asker one note inside the same session. The note binds the ask's request id, unique to that ask, and the session's causal position at the hand-off, so it answers that one ask and never another. It attests transit and never execution: it states that the herm handed the frame on, and never that the vessel read it, agreed or acted. No key signs it, so it convinces no third party; the vessel's outcome stays the one verdict on the act.

''What the ruling leaves standing.'' A party that needs to audit relay delivery after its asker vanishes holds nothing here; that need, shown concrete, re-opens the delivery receipt toward a signed note that names exactly what the relay attests. Which verbs a sibling's standing reaches across the session rides the leg's build.

''What stays owed.'' The whole leg lands with the sibling channel at face scope (<<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road#/stride-1-spine`>>, `W9` and `W12`); none of it stands built.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu slot=`#/what-the-code-holds`>>

!! What the code holds

* ''The door, arbitration, both abandonment events and the receipt reads'': `packages/lararium-node/src/uds-channel/` (the module table: <<~ loulou uri=`lar:///ha.ka.ba/lararium/mesh/uds-channel-dam`>>); the ask book `ask-book.ts`, the reads `outcome-reads.ts`, the receipts `receipts.ts`, the dispatch road `seat.ts`. The answer ring: `packages/lararium-mesh/src/control-home.ts`. The verdict row and the effect row: `packages/lararium-mesh/src/verb-tiddler.ts` and `packages/lararium-tw5/src/verb-vm.ts`.
* ''The CLI's doors'': `packages/lares-cli/src/local-connector.ts` (`invokeLocal`, `queryReceipt`, `cancelRequest`), `packages/lares-cli/src/commands/receipt-cmd.ts`, and the refusal codes of `packages/lares-cli/src/verb-call.ts`.
* ''Owed across a herm'': the whole relay leg of #/the-relay-leg, as ruled: the ask, the cancel and the answer as a tagged inner frame under the sibling session's seal, and the herm's bare delivery note bound to the request id and the session's causal position. Caller: the sibling channel at face scope.

<<~/ahu>>

